window.CHTML=window.CHTML||{};window.CHTML.cb=window.CHTML.cb||{};window.CHTML.cb[21]={"h":"<h1 data-b=\"1\" id=\"b1\">Wooldridge, Chapter 8: System Instrumental Variables and GMM</h1>\n<div class=\"box keybox\"><div class=\"bt\">Source line</div>\n<p data-b=\"2\"> Wooldridge (2010), Chapter 8, “System estimation by instrumental variables”, printed pp. 183–207. I read Sections 8.1–8.3 (pp. 183–198) closely, and used 8.5 (testing, pp. 199–202) through the way Chapter 14 refers to it. Section 8.6 (optimal instruments, p. 202) and Chapter 9 (simultaneous equations) are not covered. The proofs below follow Wooldridge’s statements; where he writes “we will not prove this”, the algebra is mine.</p>\n</div>\n<h2 data-b=\"3\" id=\"b3\">Why a system?</h2>\n<p data-b=\"4\">A panel with <math display=\"inline\"><mi>T</mi></math> periods is a system of <math display=\"inline\"><mi>T</mi></math> equations, one per period, for each cross-section unit <math display=\"inline\"><mi>i</mi></math> (pp. 183–186). So are SUR models and simultaneous equations. Wooldridge’s general linear system is <span class=\"math display\">$$y_i=X_i\\beta+u_i,\n\\;\\;\\text{\\small(21.1)}$$</span> where <math display=\"inline\"><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></math> and <math display=\"inline\"><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></math> are <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>×</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> and <math display=\"inline\"><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></math> is <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>×</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow></math>, <math display=\"inline\"><mi>β</mi></math> is <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>×</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> and the data are i.i.d. across <math display=\"inline\"><mi>i</mi></math> (equation 8.11). A <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>×</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></math> matrix of instruments <math display=\"inline\"><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></math> supplies the identifying information. Two assumptions:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><p data-b=\"5\"><strong>SIV.1:</strong> <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>E</mi><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mi>′</mi><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> (the <math display=\"inline\"><mi>L</mi></math> orthogonality conditions).</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"6\"><strong>SIV.2 (rank condition):</strong> <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">rank</mtext><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><mi>E</mi><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mi>′</mi><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow></math>.</p></li>\n</ul>\n<p data-b=\"7\"><strong>Dynamic panels are the application</strong> that matters for you. Setting <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> (first differences) and letting row <math display=\"inline\"><mi>t</mi></math> of <math display=\"inline\"><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></math> contain different instruments at different dates (the matrix in Wooldridge’s equation 11.16, Chapter 23) turns Anderson–Hsiao, Arellano–Bond and Blundell–Bond into special cases of this chapter.</p>\n<h2 data-b=\"8\" id=\"b8\">The GMM estimator: derivation</h2>\n<p data-b=\"9\">Under SIV.1 and SIV.2, <math display=\"inline\"><mi>β</mi></math> is the unique solution of the population moment equation <span class=\"math display\">$$E[Z_i'(y_i-X_i\\beta)]=0\\qquad(8.19).\n\\;\\;\\text{\\small(21.2)}$$</span> <em>Uniqueness:</em> if <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow></math>, then <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>E</mi><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">[</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mi>′</mi><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mi>b</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">]</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>E</mi><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mi>′</mi><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>b</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> because <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>E</mi><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mi>′</mi><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow></math> has full column rank (equation 8.20).</p>\n<h5 data-b=\"10\" id=\"b10\">Case <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow></math>.</h5>\n<p data-b=\"11\">Replace the expectation by the sample average: <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>N</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>∑</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mi>′</mi><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mover><mi>β</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂</mo></mover><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, giving <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mi>β</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mi>′</mi><mi>X</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mi>Z</mi><mi>′</mi><mi>Y</mi></mrow></math> (8.22), the <em>system IV</em> estimator.</p>\n<h5 data-b=\"12\" id=\"b12\">Case <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow></math>.</h5>\n<p data-b=\"13\">The <math display=\"inline\"><mi>L</mi></math> sample equations <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>N</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>∑</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mi>′</mi><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mi>b</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> generally have no exact solution. Choose <math display=\"inline\"><mi>b</mi></math> to make them small in a quadratic form with a weighting matrix <math display=\"inline\"><mover><mi>W</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂</mo></mover></math> (<math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>×</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></math>, symmetric, p.s.d.): <span class=\"math display\">$$\\hat\\beta=\\arg\\min_b\\Big[\\sum_iZ_i'(y_i-X_ib)\\Big]'\\hat W\\Big[\\sum_iZ_i'(y_i-X_ib)\\Big]\\qquad(8.23).\n\\;\\;\\text{\\small(21.3)}$$</span> By the first-order condition for a quadratic form (Chapter 19), the closed form is <span class=\"math display\">$$\\hat\\beta=(X'Z\\hat WZ'X)^{-1}(X'Z\\hat WZ'Y)\\qquad(8.24).\n\\;\\;\\text{\\small(21.4)}$$</span> Different <math display=\"inline\"><mover><mi>W</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂</mo></mover></math> give different estimators, all consistent. When <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow></math>, <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>X</mi><mi>′</mi><mi>Z</mi></mrow></math> is square and invertible and <math display=\"inline\"><mover><mi>W</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂</mo></mover></math> cancels.</p>\n<h5 data-b=\"14\" id=\"b14\">Assumption SIV.3:</h5>\n<p data-b=\"15\"><math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mi>W</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂</mo></mover><mover><mo>→</mo><mi>p</mi></mover><mi>W</mi></mrow></math>, a constant, symmetric, p.d. matrix.</p>\n<div class=\"box thm\">\n<p data-b=\"16\"><strong>Result 21.1 (Wooldridge Theorem 8.1: consistency).</strong> Under SIV.1–SIV.3, <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mi>β</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂</mo></mover><mover><mo>→</mo><mi>p</mi></mover><mi>β</mi></mrow></math>.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"proof\">\n<p data-b=\"17\"><em>Proof.</em> Substitute <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>X</mi><mi>β</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> into (21.4): <math display=\"block\"><mrow><mover><mi>β</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>+</mo><mo form=\"prefix\" maxsize=\"1.2\" minsize=\"1.2\" stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mi>′</mi><mi>Z</mi><mi>/</mi><mi>N</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mover><mi>W</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂</mo></mover><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mi>′</mi><mi>X</mi><mi>/</mi><mi>N</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><msup><mo form=\"postfix\" maxsize=\"1.2\" minsize=\"1.2\" stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mi>′</mi><mi>Z</mi><mi>/</mi><mi>N</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mover><mi>W</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂</mo></mover><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mi>′</mi><mi>u</mi><mi>/</mi><mi>N</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mi>.</mi></mrow></math> Let <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>E</mi><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mi>′</mi><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow></math>. The WLLN gives <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mi>′</mi><mi>X</mi><mi>/</mi><mi>N</mi><mover><mo>→</mo><mi>p</mi></mover><mi>C</mi></mrow></math> and <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mi>′</mi><mi>u</mi><mi>/</mi><mi>N</mi><mover><mo>→</mo><mi>p</mi></mover><mi>E</mi><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mi>′</mi><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>. The matrix <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mi>′</mi><mi>W</mi><mi>C</mi></mrow></math> is p.d. because <math display=\"inline\"><mi>C</mi></math> has rank <math display=\"inline\"><mi>K</mi></math> and <math display=\"inline\"><mi>W</mi></math> is p.d. By Slutsky, <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mi>β</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂</mo></mover><mo>−</mo><mi>β</mi><mover><mo>→</mo><mi>p</mi></mover><msup><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mi>′</mi><mi>W</mi><mi>C</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mi>C</mi><mi>′</mi><mi>W</mi><mo>⋅</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>. ◻</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"box thm\">\n<p data-b=\"18\"><strong>Result 21.2 (Wooldridge Theorem 8.2: asymptotic normality).</strong> Let <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Λ</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>E</mi><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mi>′</mi><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mi>′</mi><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow></math> (equation 8.26). Under SIV.1–SIV.3, <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><msqrt><mi>N</mi></msqrt><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mover><mi>β</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂</mo></mover><mo>−</mo><mi>β</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mover><mo>→</mo><mi>d</mi></mover><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">Normal</mtext><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>W</mi></msub><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow></math> with <math display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>W</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mi>′</mi><mi>W</mi><mi>C</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mi>C</mi><mi>′</mi><mi>W</mi><mi>Λ</mi><mi>W</mi><mi>C</mi><msup><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mi>′</mi><mi>W</mi><mi>C</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mspace width=\"2.0em\"></mspace><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mn>8.25</mn><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mi>.</mi></mrow></math></p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"proof\">\n<p data-b=\"19\"><em>Proof.</em> From the display in Theorem 21.1, <math display=\"block\"><mrow><msqrt><mi>N</mi></msqrt><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mover><mi>β</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂</mo></mover><mo>−</mo><mi>β</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">[</mo><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mi>′</mi><mi>Z</mi><mi>/</mi><mi>N</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mover><mi>W</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂</mo></mover><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mi>′</mi><mi>X</mi><mi>/</mi><mi>N</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">]</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mi>′</mi><mi>Z</mi><mi>/</mi><mi>N</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mover><mi>W</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂</mo></mover><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msup><mi>N</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mi>/</mi><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>∑</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mi>′</mi><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mi>.</mi></mrow></math> The vectors <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mi>′</mi><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> are i.i.d., have mean zero and variance <math display=\"inline\"><mi>Λ</mi></math>, so the CLT gives <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>N</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mi>/</mi><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>∑</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mi>′</mi><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mover><mo>→</mo><mi>d</mi></mover><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">Normal</mtext><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>Λ</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow></math>. Slutsky and the rule <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">Var</mtext><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mi>q</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>A</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">Var</mtext><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mi>A</mi><mi>′</mi></mrow></math> with <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mi>′</mi><mi>W</mi><mi>C</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mi>C</mi><mi>′</mi><mi>W</mi></mrow></math> give the result. (Wooldridge states this and omits the proof.) ◻</p>\n</div>\n<p data-b=\"20\">A consistent estimate is obtained by replacing <math display=\"inline\"><mi>C</mi></math> with <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mi>′</mi><mi>X</mi><mi>/</mi><mi>N</mi></mrow></math>, <math display=\"inline\"><mi>W</mi></math> with <math display=\"inline\"><mover><mi>W</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂</mo></mover></math> and <math display=\"inline\"><mi>Λ</mi></math> with <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mi>Λ</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>∑</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mi>′</mi><msub><mover><mi>u</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mover><mi>u</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mi>′</mi><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math>, where <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mi>u</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mover><mi>β</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̃</mo></mover></mrow></math> uses residuals from any consistent preliminary estimator (equation 8.27).</p>\n<h2 data-b=\"21\" id=\"b21\">System 2SLS</h2>\n<p data-b=\"22\">Choosing <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mi>W</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mi>′</mi><mi>Z</mi><mi>/</mi><mi>N</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mo form=\"prefix\" maxsize=\"1.2\" minsize=\"1.2\" stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>∑</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mi>′</mi><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msup><mo form=\"postfix\" maxsize=\"1.2\" minsize=\"1.2\" stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math> (8.28) gives <math display=\"block\"><mrow><mover><mi>β</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mo form=\"prefix\" maxsize=\"1.2\" minsize=\"1.2\" stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>X</mi><mi>′</mi><mi>Z</mi><msup><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mi>′</mi><mi>Z</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mi>Z</mi><mi>′</mi><mi>X</mi><msup><mo form=\"postfix\" maxsize=\"1.2\" minsize=\"1.2\" stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mi>X</mi><mi>′</mi><mi>Z</mi><msup><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mi>′</mi><mi>Z</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mi>Z</mi><mi>′</mi><mi>Y</mi><mspace width=\"2.0em\"></mspace><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mn>8.29</mn><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></math> which has the form of single-equation 2SLS (Theorem 20.1) with <math display=\"inline\"><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>Z</mi></msub></math> built from the stacked <math display=\"inline\"><mi>Z</mi></math>. It requires no estimate of the error covariance and is therefore robust, but it is generally inefficient.</p>\n<h2 data-b=\"23\" id=\"b23\">The optimal weighting matrix</h2>\n<div class=\"box thm\">\n<p data-b=\"24\"><strong>Result 21.3 (Wooldridge Theorem 8.3).</strong> Under SIV.1–SIV.3, with <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>Λ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math> (assumption SIV.4), the asymptotic variance is <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>o</mi><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mi>′</mi><msup><mi>Λ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mi>C</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math> and <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>W</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>o</mi><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is p.s.d. for every p.d. <math display=\"inline\"><mi>W</mi></math>.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"proof\">\n<p data-b=\"25\"><em>Proof.</em> (The following proof is mine.) Put <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mi>′</mi><mi>W</mi><mi>C</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mi>C</mi><mi>′</mi><mi>W</mi></mrow></math> and <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mi>′</mi><msup><mi>Λ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mi>C</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mi>C</mi><mi>′</mi><msup><mi>Λ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math> (<math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>×</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></math> matrices), so <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>W</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>A</mi><mi>Λ</mi><mi>A</mi><mi>′</mi></mrow></math> and <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>o</mi><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>B</mi><mi>Λ</mi><mi>B</mi><mi>′</mi></mrow></math>. Compute <math display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>B</mi><mi>Λ</mi><mi>A</mi><mi>′</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mi>′</mi><msup><mi>Λ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mi>C</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mi>C</mi><mi>′</mi><msup><mi>Λ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mi>Λ</mi><mi>W</mi><mi>C</mi><msup><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mi>′</mi><mi>W</mi><mi>C</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mi>′</mi><msup><mi>Λ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mi>C</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mi>B</mi><mi>Λ</mi><mi>B</mi><mi>′</mi><mi>.</mi></mrow></math> Therefore <math display=\"block\"><mrow><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>B</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mi>Λ</mi><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>B</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mi>′</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>A</mi><mi>Λ</mi><mi>A</mi><mi>′</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>A</mi><mi>Λ</mi><mi>B</mi><mi>′</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>B</mi><mi>Λ</mi><mi>A</mi><mi>′</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>B</mi><mi>Λ</mi><mi>B</mi><mi>′</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>W</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>o</mi><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>o</mi><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>o</mi><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>W</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>o</mi><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo></mrow></math> using <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mi>Λ</mi><mi>B</mi><mi>′</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>B</mi><mi>Λ</mi><mi>A</mi><mi>′</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mi>′</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>o</mi><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> (<math display=\"inline\"><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>o</mi><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub></math> is symmetric). The left-hand side is of the form <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>D</mi><mi>Λ</mi><mi>D</mi><mi>′</mi></mrow></math> with <math display=\"inline\"><mi>Λ</mi></math> p.s.d., hence p.s.d. Therefore <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>W</mi></msub><mo>≽</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>o</mi><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. ◻</p>\n</div>\n<p data-b=\"26\"><em>In words:</em> give less weight to moments that are noisier and to those that are redundant with others. The optimal weighting matrix is the inverse of the variance of the moments.</p>\n<figure data-b=\"27\">\n<img alt=\"Figure\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/fig/f21_opt.svg\"/>\n<figcaption><strong>Figure 21.1.</strong> A one-parameter, two-moment example (my numbers): <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>C</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mi>′</mi></mrow></math>, <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Λ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"center\" style=\"text-align: center\"><mn>1</mn></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"center\" style=\"text-align: center\"><mn>0.5</mn></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"center\" style=\"text-align: center\"><mn>0.5</mn></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"center\" style=\"text-align: center\"><mn>4</mn></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow></math>. Weighting the moments equally (<math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.5</mn></mrow></math>, which is the same as <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow></math>) gives an asymptotic variance of 1.50; the optimum <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mi>/</mi><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mi>′</mi><msup><mi>Λ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mi>G</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0.9375</mn></mrow></math> puts weight <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>≈</mo><mn>0.88</mn></mrow></math> on the precise first moment.</figcaption>\n</figure>\n<h5 data-b=\"28\" id=\"b28\">Feasible two-step GMM (Wooldridge, p. 193).</h5>\n<ol>\n<li><p data-b=\"29\">Estimate by system 2SLS (any consistent <math display=\"inline\"><mover><mi>W</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂</mo></mover></math>); compute residuals <math display=\"inline\"><msub><mover><mi>u</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub></math>.</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"30\">Form <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mi>Λ</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>∑</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mi>′</mi><msub><mover><mi>u</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mover><mi>u</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mi>′</mi><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> and set <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mi>W</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><msup><mover><mi>Λ</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂</mo></mover><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math>.</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"31\">Re-estimate (21.4). Asymptotic variance: <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mi>′</mi><mi>Z</mi><mi>/</mi><mi>N</mi><mspace width=\"0.278em\"></mspace><msup><mover><mi>Λ</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂</mo></mover><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mspace width=\"0.278em\"></mspace><mi>Z</mi><mi>′</mi><mi>X</mi><mi>/</mi><mi>N</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mi>/</mi><mi>N</mi></mrow></math>.</p></li>\n</ol>\n<h2 data-b=\"32\" id=\"b32\">Three-stage least squares and the homoskedasticity case</h2>\n<p data-b=\"33\">If the <em>system homoskedasticity assumption</em> SIV.5 holds, <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>E</mi><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mi>′</mi><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mi>′</mi><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>E</mi><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mi>′</mi><mi>Ω</mi><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow></math> with <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Ω</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>E</mi><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mi>′</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow></math>, then <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mi>Λ</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>∑</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mi>′</mi><mover><mi>Ω</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂</mo></mover><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math>, and the efficient GMM estimator is the (GMM) <strong>three-stage least squares</strong> estimator, built from system-2SLS residuals <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mi>Ω</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>∑</mo><msub><mover><mi>u</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mover><mi>u</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mi>′</mi></mrow></math> (pp. 194–198, Theorem 8.4). With the same instruments in every equation, <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo>⊗</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math>, 3SLS collapses to equation-by-equation 2SLS when there are no cross-equation restrictions. Wooldridge warns (pp. 196–198) that the <em>traditional</em> 3SLS formula, which first transforms the instruments, differs from GMM 3SLS when instruments differ across equations, and in Section 11.1.1 that the traditional form is <em>not consistent</em> when the instruments are sequentially, rather than strictly, exogenous (p. 305). That is the case of dynamic panels.</p>\n<h2 data-b=\"34\" id=\"b34\">Testing</h2>\n<h5 data-b=\"35\" id=\"b35\">Overidentifying restrictions (Section 8.5.2, p. 201).</h5>\n<p data-b=\"36\">Take the optimal estimator (<math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mi>W</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><msup><mover><mi>Λ</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂</mo></mover><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math>) and the statistic <span class=\"math display\">$$J=N\\,\\Big[N^{-1}\\sum Z_i'\\hat u_i\\Big]'\\hat\\Lambda^{-1}\\Big[N^{-1}\\sum Z_i'\\hat u_i\\Big]=\\hat g'\\hat\\Lambda^{-1}\\hat g,\n\\;\\;\\text{\\small(21.5)}$$</span> which is the minimised value of the GMM objective. <strong>Under the null that all <math display=\"inline\"><mi>L</mi></math> moment conditions hold, <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>J</mi><mover><mo>→</mo><mi>d</mi></mover><msubsup><mi>χ</mi><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></math></strong> (Wooldridge’s equation 8.49).</p>\n<div class=\"proof\">\n<p data-b=\"37\"><em>Derivation (mine).</em> Let <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mi>g</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mi>/</mi><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>∑</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mi>′</mi><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mover><mi>β</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂</mo></mover><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow></math> and <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mi>/</mi><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>∑</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mi>′</mi><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mover><mo>→</mo><mi>d</mi></mover><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">Normal</mtext><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>Λ</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow></math>. Since <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mi>g</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>∑</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mi>′</mi><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><msqrt><mi>N</mi></msqrt><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mover><mi>β</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂</mo></mover><mo>−</mo><mi>β</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow></math> and the optimal estimator has <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><msqrt><mi>N</mi></msqrt><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mover><mi>β</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂</mo></mover><mo>−</mo><mi>β</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mi>′</mi><msup><mi>Λ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mi>C</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mi>C</mi><mi>′</mi><msup><mi>Λ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mi>s</mi><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow></math>, we have <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mi>g</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mi>M</mi><mi>s</mi><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow></math> with <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>I</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>C</mi><msup><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mi>′</mi><msup><mi>Λ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mi>C</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mi>C</mi><mi>′</mi><msup><mi>Λ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math>. Write <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>Λ</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mi>/</mi><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mi>ξ</mi></mrow></math> with <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>ξ</mi><mo>∼</mo><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">Normal</mtext><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow></math> and <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mi>C</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̃</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>Λ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mi>/</mi><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mi>C</mi></mrow></math>. Then <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>Λ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mi>/</mi><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mover><mi>g</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>I</mi><mo>−</mo><mover><mi>C</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̃</mo></mover><msup><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mover><mi>C</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̃</mo></mover><mi>′</mi><mover><mi>C</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̃</mo></mover><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mover><mi>C</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̃</mo></mover><mi>′</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mi>ξ</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mover><mi>C</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̃</mo></mover></msub><mi>ξ</mi></mrow></math>, and <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>=</mo><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"false\">∥</mo><msup><mi>Λ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mi>/</mi><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mover><mi>g</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂</mo></mover><msup><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"false\">∥</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mi>ξ</mi><mi>′</mi><msub><mi>M</mi><mover><mi>C</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̃</mo></mover></msub><mi>ξ</mi></mrow></math>. <math display=\"inline\"><msub><mi>M</mi><mover><mi>C</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̃</mo></mover></msub></math> is a symmetric idempotent matrix of rank <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow></math> (Theorem 19.1), so <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>∼</mo><msubsup><mi>χ</mi><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></math>. ◻</p>\n</div>\n<p data-b=\"38\"><em>Reading the result:</em> the first <math display=\"inline\"><mi>K</mi></math> directions in moment space are used up in estimating <math display=\"inline\"><mi>β</mi></math>; only the remaining <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow></math> directions are free to disagree with zero.</p>\n<h5 data-b=\"39\" id=\"b39\">Testing a subset of the moments (the “difference-in-<math display=\"inline\"><mi>J</mi></math>” test).</h5>\n<p data-b=\"40\">Wooldridge’s Section 8.5.1 gives the distance (criterion-function) statistic (8.45): estimate with all <math display=\"inline\"><mi>L</mi></math> moments and with <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></math> of them, <em>using the same weighting matrix</em>, and compare the minimised objectives; the difference is <math display=\"inline\"><msubsup><mi>χ</mi><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup></math> under the null that the extra moments are valid. This is the test used in Chapters 24–25 to check the extra moment conditions of system GMM.</p>\n<h5 data-b=\"41\" id=\"b41\">Wald tests.</h5>\n<p data-b=\"42\">For <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>:</mo><mi>R</mi><mi>β</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></math>, <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mover><mi>β</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂</mo></mover><mo>−</mo><mi>r</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mi>′</mi><msup><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">[</mo><mi>R</mi><mover><mi>V</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂</mo></mover><mi>R</mi><mi>′</mi><mi>/</mi><mi>N</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">]</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mover><mi>β</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂</mo></mover><mo>−</mo><mi>r</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>∼</mo><msubsup><mi>χ</mi><mrow><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">rank</mtext><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></math> (equation 8.48).</p>\n<h2 data-b=\"43\" id=\"b43\">Choosing an estimator (Section 8.4, p. 198)</h2>\n<table data-b=\"44\">\n<caption><strong>Table 21.1.</strong> Weighting-matrix choices. The last row is where small-sample trouble comes from: you are estimating an <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>×</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></math> matrix from <math display=\"inline\"><mi>N</mi></math> clusters.</caption>\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\"><strong>Estimator</strong></th>\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\"><strong>When it is efficient</strong></th>\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\"><strong>Main risk</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">System 2SLS</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Never optimal in general; fine as a robust first step</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Inefficient under heteroskedasticity or serial correlation</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">GMM 3SLS (SIV.5)</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Under system homoskedasticity</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Inconsistent variance estimates if SIV.5 fails</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Optimal two-step GMM (<math display=\"inline\"><msup><mover><mi>Λ</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂</mo></mover><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></math>)</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Always, asymptotically</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\"><math display=\"inline\"><mover><mi>Λ</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂</mo></mover></math> has many elements; estimated imprecisely when <math display=\"inline\"><mi>L</mi></math> is large relative to <math display=\"inline\"><mi>N</mi></math> (<em>the key warning of Chapters 24–25</em>)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h2 data-b=\"45\" id=\"b45\">Common misreadings</h2>\n<div class=\"box plainbox\"><div class=\"bt\">Four things people get wrong</div>\n<p data-b=\"46\"></p>\n<ol>\n<li><p data-b=\"47\">“GMM is a different method from IV.” IV, 2SLS, 3SLS and system IV are GMM with particular weighting matrices.</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"48\">“The efficient GMM estimator is always best.” It is best asymptotically; in finite samples the estimated <math display=\"inline\"><msup><mover><mi>Λ</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂</mo></mover><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></math> adds noise, and system 2SLS can win.</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"49\">“The <math display=\"inline\"><mi>J</mi></math> test has <math display=\"inline\"><mi>L</mi></math> degrees of freedom.” It has <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow></math>.</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"50\">“<math display=\"inline\"><mi>J</mi></math> fails to reject, so the model is right.” It is a test of the <em>over</em>identifying restrictions only; exact identification is untestable.</p></li>\n</ol>\n</div>\n<h2 data-b=\"51\" id=\"b51\">What it means for the thesis</h2>\n<p data-b=\"52\"><em>My inference.</em> Everything you will do with dynamic panel GMM (Chapters 23–25) is an instance of equations (21.3)–(21.5); the only new ingredient is which columns go into <math display=\"inline\"><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></math>. If you can write down <math display=\"inline\"><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></math> for your model you can read any xtabond2 or <code>pgmm</code> output: the table shows <math display=\"inline\"><mover><mi>β</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂</mo></mover></math>, standard errors from <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mi>′</mi><msup><mover><mi>Λ</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂</mo></mover><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mi>C</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mi>/</mi><mi>N</mi></mrow></math>, the number of instruments <math display=\"inline\"><mi>L</mi></math> and the <math display=\"inline\"><mi>J</mi></math> statistic with <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow></math> degrees of freedom.</p>\n<div class=\"box verdictbox\"><div class=\"bt\">Do you need the original?</div>\n<p data-b=\"53\"> <strong>For the logic and proofs of Theorems 8.1–8.3 and the <math display=\"inline\"><mi>J</mi></math> test: no.</strong><br/>\n<strong>Read Section 8.3.5</strong> (pp. 196–198, GMM 3SLS versus traditional 3SLS) in the original if you intend to estimate with a system routine rather than a dynamic-panel command.<br/>\n<strong>Read Section 8.6</strong> (optimal instruments) only if you plan to use nonlinear efficiency arguments; I did not read it.</p>\n</div>\n<h2 class=\"unnumbered\" data-b=\"54\" id=\"b54\">Check yourself</h2>\n<div class=\"qlist\">\n<p data-b=\"55\">State SIV.1 and SIV.2.</p>\n<p data-b=\"56\">What is the GMM estimator in closed form for a given <math display=\"inline\"><mover><mi>W</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂</mo></mover></math>? What is the optimal <math display=\"inline\"><mover><mi>W</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂</mo></mover></math>?</p>\n<p data-b=\"57\">What is the null distribution of <math display=\"inline\"><mi>J</mi></math>, and its degrees of freedom?</p>\n<p data-b=\"58\">Why is the GMM estimator consistent for any p.d. <math display=\"inline\"><mi>W</mi></math>?</p>\n<p data-b=\"59\">Explain why <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mi>W</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mi>′</mi><mi>Z</mi><mi>/</mi><mi>N</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math> gives 2SLS.</p>\n<p data-b=\"60\">Why does the optimal weighting matrix depend on the variance of the <em>moments</em>, not of the errors?</p>\n<p data-b=\"61\">Why can adding moment conditions never reduce asymptotic efficiency (with the optimal weight), yet hurt in small samples?</p>\n<p data-b=\"62\">Compute the asymptotic variance for <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow></math> and for <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>Λ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math> in the example of Figure 21.1, with <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mi>′</mi></mrow></math> and <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Λ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"center\" style=\"text-align: center\"><mn>2</mn></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"center\" style=\"text-align: center\"><mn>1</mn></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"center\" style=\"text-align: center\"><mn>1</mn></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"center\" style=\"text-align: center\"><mn>2</mn></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p data-b=\"63\">A dynamic panel model has 14 moment conditions and 3 parameters. What are the degrees of freedom of <math display=\"inline\"><mi>J</mi></math>? If <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>26</mn></mrow></math>, what is the decision at 5%?</p>\n<p data-b=\"64\">Suppose <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow></math>. What is <math display=\"inline\"><mi>J</mi></math>? Why?</p>\n<p data-b=\"65\">“Because two-step GMM is efficient, always use it.” Respond.</p>\n<p data-b=\"66\">“GMM requires fewer assumptions than maximum likelihood, so it is always preferable.” Discuss.</p>\n</div>\n<h3 class=\"unnumbered\" data-b=\"67\" id=\"b67\">Answer sketches</h3>\n<div class=\"alist\">\n<p data-b=\"68\"><math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>E</mi><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mi>′</mi><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>; <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">rank</mtext><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><mi>E</mi><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mi>′</mi><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p data-b=\"69\"><math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mi>β</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mi>′</mi><mi>Z</mi><mover><mi>W</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂</mo></mover><mi>Z</mi><mi>′</mi><mi>X</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mi>X</mi><mi>′</mi><mi>Z</mi><mover><mi>W</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂</mo></mover><mi>Z</mi><mi>′</mi><mi>Y</mi></mrow></math>; <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mi>W</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂</mo></mover><mover><mo>→</mo><mi>p</mi></mover><msup><mi>Λ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p data-b=\"70\"><math display=\"inline\"><msubsup><mi>χ</mi><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup></math>.</p>\n<p data-b=\"71\">Because the sample moments converge to zero at the truth and <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mi>′</mi><mi>W</mi><mi>C</mi></mrow></math> is invertible for any p.d. <math display=\"inline\"><mi>W</mi></math>.</p>\n<p data-b=\"72\">Substituting <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mi>W</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mi>′</mi><mi>Z</mi><mi>/</mi><mi>N</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math> in (21.4) gives <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">[</mo><mi>X</mi><mi>′</mi><mi>Z</mi><msup><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mi>′</mi><mi>Z</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mi>Z</mi><mi>′</mi><mi>X</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">]</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mi>X</mi><mi>′</mi><mi>Z</mi><msup><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mi>′</mi><mi>Z</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mi>Z</mi><mi>′</mi><mi>Y</mi></mrow></math>, the 2SLS formula.</p>\n<p data-b=\"73\">Because the estimator is built from the moments; the precision of a moment, not of an error, decides its weight.</p>\n<p data-b=\"74\">With the optimal weight an extra moment can be given weight zero, so asymptotic variance cannot rise; in finite samples <math display=\"inline\"><mover><mi>Λ</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂</mo></mover></math> must be estimated with more elements and the resulting noise biases the estimate.</p>\n<p data-b=\"75\"><math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow></math>: <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>G</mi><mi>′</mi><mi>Λ</mi><mi>G</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mi>Λ</mi><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mi>′</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></math>, <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>G</mi><mi>′</mi><mi>G</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>, so <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>6</mn><mi>/</mi><mn>4</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>1.5</mn></mrow></math>. Optimal: <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>Λ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"center\" style=\"text-align: center\"><mn>2</mn></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"center\" style=\"text-align: center\"><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"center\" style=\"text-align: center\"><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"center\" style=\"text-align: center\"><mn>2</mn></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow></math>, <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>G</mi><mi>′</mi><msup><mi>Λ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mi>G</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>2</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac></mrow></math>, so <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1.5</mn></mrow></math>. Equal: here the moments are exchangeable, so equal weights are already optimal.</p>\n<p data-b=\"76\"><math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>14</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>11</mn></mrow></math>; the 5% critical value of <math display=\"inline\"><msubsup><mi>χ</mi><mn>11</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></math> is 19.68; <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>26</mn><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>19.68</mn></mrow></math>, reject.</p>\n<p data-b=\"77\"><math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>: the moment equations can be solved exactly, so the minimised objective is zero.</p>\n<p data-b=\"78\">Efficiency is asymptotic; with many moments <math display=\"inline\"><mover><mi>Λ</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂</mo></mover></math> is poorly estimated, the standard errors from two-step GMM are too small, and bias grows (Chapters 24–25).</p>\n<p data-b=\"79\">GMM needs only moment conditions, so it is robust to distributional misspecification, but it is less efficient when the likelihood is correct and, with many moments, it can behave badly in small samples.</p>\n</div>\n","s":[[155,"b3"],[155,"b8"],[156,"b21"],[157,"b23"],[158,"b32"],[158,"b34"],[158,"b43"],[159,"b45"],[159,"b51"]]};